> *"Over fifty years, the vibration of strings never lies."*
> *— O Han-gyeol, piano tuner · emotion: resolve*

Every time O Han-gyeol walked down the basement corridor of the concert hall, his footsteps sounded different. On days when the old carpet laid over the concrete was damp with moisture, his steps were slightly heavier. In the dry winter, they rang out lightly. He judged the day's stage humidity by that difference. It was a habit built over forty-three years.

In his bag were a single tuning lever, a tuning fork, and nothing else. He had put away the digital tuner twenty years ago. Sometimes the numbers matched, but the sound was wrong.

---

That morning, Su-yeon Lee, the operations manager of the concert hall, came down to the basement herself. That alone was unusual. On her tablet was a paused promotional video for HanEum Tech's new product. It described how precision microphones captured the frequency of each string, and a deep learning algorithm calculated optimal tension to guide the tuner with precise numbers.

"Twelve music university practice rooms across the country completed their installation this year. There was even an announcement that the accuracy is three times higher than the human ear."

O Han-gyeol did not look up as he closed his bag's zipper.

"So."

"Before next season's contract renewal... I wanted to consider it."

Before she finished speaking, he was already climbing the corridor stairs. His silence was an answer.

---

A grand piano stood alone on the stage. O Han-gyeol opened its lid, sat down, and slowly pressed a full octave from C to B. The sound traveled through the air and settled inside the cochlea of his ear.

It was there.

On the G sharp, an octave above, one of three strings was slightly flat. It was within the software's margin of error. But O Han-gyeol knew. This hall had sunlight streaming through its south-facing window at two in the afternoon, and at that moment, the wooden stage floor expanded silently. That half-cent became a full cent at the exact moment of the day's first note.

It was not a measurement. It was what had accumulated over forty-three years of experiencing this hall's springs, summers, autumns, and winters together. No matter how accurate HanEum Tech's algorithm was, it had never once been in this hall. It did not know how much the floor would expand this afternoon.

He picked up the lever and raised the pitch by half a cent. The three strings began to breathe together.

---

When the tuning was done, pianist Min-jae Park came up onto the stage. He tapped a few keys lightly and stopped.

"The sound is different today."

O Han-gyeol spoke quietly.

"Raised it by half a cent."

Min-jae Park nodded once and began to practice. O Han-gyeol came down the stairs without looking back. He had no reason to look back.

---

Su-yeon Lee was waiting in the corridor. Her tablet was off.

"Master, we haven't decided on HanEum Tech yet."

O Han-gyeol repositioned his bag on his shoulder.

"That machine isn't bad. I use a frequency calculator sometimes."

He paused, then continued.

"But what sound this hall wants to make this afternoon—you can't ask that of something that has never been here."

Su-yeon said nothing. O Han-gyeol said nothing more. The conversation ended, still open.

The afternoon sun slanted diagonally across the corridor windows. From somewhere inside the stage, the sound of Min-jae's practice drifted out. The G sharp, raised by half a cent, held its place, at least for this afternoon.

